Crawl does mostly this and it works out OK; if you want to hit your
previous target you hit p, and if you don't, you target somebody
else. I find this (and Crawl's targeting system in general) to be
super-convenient, especially since spellcasting and targeting isn't
limited to cardinal directions. Granted it takes some getting used to
(and control issues can totally cause deaths at first) but I think
it's a feature that would improve NetHack's interface if implemented
cleanly across the board. Making it just a weird hack for polearms? More
confusing.  (though still helpful, honestly)

The big difference is that Crawl's isn't a _default_; it's a "default if
you push the 'give me the default' button." Most typos will just remind
you that you hit a useless key, unless of course you typo and hit the
default button, which will be a problem in any interface. It's certainly
better than "the magical energy is released!" and "What the hell happened?
Oh, I hit the wrong button, and NetHack decided to randomly harsh me."

If you're interested in roguelike interface design, I encourage you to
check out what Crawl does, especially with macros and targeting. The
interface isn't NetHack's, and NetHack _shouldn't_ look the same, but
being able to macro things would make my wrists _much happier_...

Still pushing the Crawl interface, :)

